---
import DraftBench from "../../../components/studies/derelict-draft-two/DraftBench.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";
import "@void-drift/core/styles/buttons.css";

const study = studies.find(
	(entry) => entry.slug === "derelict-draft-two-2026-11-06",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<aside aria-label="Study outcome">
		<h2>Aborted / Stopped after 02.2</h2>
		<p>
			This study was aborted on 6 November 2026 at the designer’s request. Work
			through 02.2 was valuable: the hull translation, enlarged hollow energy
			square, broad outer glow and initial ship comparison are retained. Size
			was not settled. The subsequent camera comparison was unusable, and
			repeated corrections followed by process discussion stopped the
			exploration from progressing. The designer assessed this as a model-fault
			loop. The rejected 02.3 viewer has been removed. Readiness and
			implementation scope were not reached.
		</p>
	</aside>
	<section>
		<h2>01 / Carry the drawing into the game</h2>
		<p>
			A giant shard of a monumental space construct: two visible hulls, joined
			underneath by a darker structure of the same material. Unequal spear-like
			ends, long planes and the open interior carry its identity. It belongs to
			a different family from the player’s ships. The supplied second draft
			establishes the silhouette and colour language for this cycle.
		</p>
		<p>
			A hollow brand-colour square rotates inside and leaves light on the
			effects layer. The immediate question is how faithfully the drawing
			survives the game’s rendering and smaller viewing sizes. The hull paths
			are held fixed during this first translation. Connecting structure,
			energy, and eventual physical splinters belong to the idea; rupture
			choreography, collision geometry and encounter scale remain open.
		</p>
		<p>
			The
			<a href="/lab/studies/derelicts-2026-11-06">previous stopped study</a>
			remains separate. Its proposed corrections do not define this draft.
		</p>
	</section>
	<details id="translation" open>
		<summary>02.1 / Same geometry, live light</summary>
		<h2>02.1 / Same geometry, live light</h2>
		<p>
			The Canvas example below uses the supplied SVG’s actual paths and base
			colours, including its warm-to-cool hue overlay. The dark connecting bed
			is a flat material fill; the original baked illumination is replaced by
			light from the rotating square. The square uses the current brand token,
			which is brighter than the reference green.
		</p>
		<DraftBench client:load />
		<p>
			The inner square is enlarged by 20% from the initial translation, with a
			broad halo reaching into the surrounding hulls. The rotating square keeps
			a black, empty centre while the broad outer glow is retained. Rotation is
			provisionally one turn in twelve seconds. Glow uses the production
			persistent FX surface and its shared decay. Pause freezes both; switching
			glow off lets the existing light fade while the solid square remains.
			Reset clears the light history. This is a stationary artwork probe, not a
			flight or breakup simulation.
		</p>
		<p>
			The 36 px view probes the existing wreck’s approximate diameter, not an
			agreed size for a monumental shard. The 72 and 180 px views leave that
			design choice visible. Labels describe logical canvas pixels at the
			bench’s native width.
		</p>
		<details>
			<summary>Supplied second draft / visual anchor</summary>
			<img
				src="/studies/derelict-draft-two-2026-11-06/reference.png"
				width="694"
				height="1198"
				alt="Inkscape draft: two long faceted hulls with pale mint, grey and warm upper planes, a dark connecting bed and a luminous hollow green square inside."
			>
			<p>
				<a href="/studies/derelict-draft-two-2026-11-06/source.svg"
					>Original Inkscape vector</a
				>. Preserved as supplied. The interactive example draws extracted
				geometry, not this image.
			</p>
		</details>
		<p>
			Import feasibility is demonstrated for the intact drawing and a separate
			energy emitter. The enlarged square, outer glow and black centre received
			positive visual review. Desired scale remains unresolved. The hue overlay
			is retained as an explicit study experiment; adopting it for production
			would need to resolve the flat-fill hull principle. No production
			art-direction change is established here.
		</p>
	</details>
	<section id="ship-scale">
		<h2>02.2 / A ship beside a monumental remnant</h2>
		<p>
			The enlarged energy square, broad outer glow and black centre are retained
			following visual review. This comparison changes only the derelict’s size.
			Each candidate sits beside the same production LHI Wasp, drawn with its
			starter fittings and the game’s 0.65 flight scale. All three pairs share
			one viewing scale; the ship stays the same size.
		</p>
		<DraftBench
			client:load
			comparison
			label="Derelict and Wasp scale comparison"
		/>
		<p>
			Left: 120 px long. Middle: 240 px. Right: 420 px. The Wasp faces upward
			beside each remnant, with engines idle. These are candidate on-screen
			sizes at camera zoom 1, not fixed screen sizes across the game’s automatic
			zoom. The middle candidate is a starting hypothesis for a substantial
			encounter; the right tests whether the stronger sense of scale merits
			occupying more of the view.
		</p>
		<p>
			This stationary comparison establishes visual proportion only. Passing
			distance, automatic camera framing, collision boundaries and breakup still
			need a flight trial after a size direction is chosen.
		</p>
	</section>
</StudyLayout>
<style>
img {
	display: block;
	max-height: 700px;
	width: auto;
	max-width: 100%;
	margin-block: var(--spacing-lg);
}
details {
	margin-block: var(--spacing-xl);
}
</style>
